# =============================================================================
# GridPro's aerofoil blocking refined the way its OWN FIGURES arrange it:
# THREE CONCENTRIC RINGS x SIXTEEN CIRCUMFERENTIAL SECTORS, 58 blocks.
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_rings.py
#   ./run.sh -conf docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_rings.dat
#
# A RESEARCH HARNESS, not a shipped case: it lives beside
# docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method.md (section 9) rather than in config/,
# nothing in examples/ or config/ refers to it, and no gate covers it.
#
# It does NOT supersede gridpro_tworing.dat beside it. That one is the 14-block
# case section 8 measures, which is the tutorial's own block count; this one is
# the arrangement the two single-body figures in 'Grid Generation: an Art or
# Science?' show -- concentric rings by many sectors -- and section 9e measures
# what the difference costs. It is better in the field and worse at the wall.
#
# Everything structural is still GridPro's: a far-field CIRCLE of radius 3
# centred ON THE TRAILING EDGE, so the three internal surfaces radiating from
# the trailing edge are RADII of it, and those three BISECT THE EXTERIOR ANGLE
# EQUALLY. Section 9d is why there are three and not five.
#
# AND THE BLOCK BOUNDARIES ARE CURVES, which is section 10. Every outer radial
# is the arc that leaves its inner ring along that ring's own normal, and the
# trailing-edge tail is the ring's own curve continued rather than a box of
# chords -- both DERIVED, each being the unique arc through two points with a
# prescribed tangent at one of them. They cannot be relaxed into existence here:
# with every interior line left free the smoother reports diverged=1 at sweep 13
# and folds six cells if forced to 20, which section 10b measures. Put either
# back with --chord-radials / --chord-tail.
# =============================================================================

MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_rings.json

# The aerofoil the C-grid case already ships: two segments, upper and lower,
# split at the leading edge because that is where a block corner sits. There is
# NO far-field geometry -- every ring, the circle included, is declared.
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/naca0012_cgrid.dat

BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001
MB_SPLIT_QUADS 1

# The FALLBACK, and here it is load bearing rather than a safety net: the outer
# ring's arcs carry no binding (an analytic curve has no sidecar to read a
# condition off), so this is where their condition comes from. The aerofoil's
# two sides are bound and take 'wall' off their own sidecar.
BC_GEOM farfield

# The repo default of 20 sweeps DIVERGES on this family -- section 8g measured
# it stopping at sweep 7 and reporting diverged=1 -- so the cap is five, which
# is where the wall height is held and nothing has turned.
MB_SMOOTH_ITERS 5

EXPORT_VTK 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/gridpro_rings/mesh_gridpro_rings.vtk
